// ============================================================================
// The prologue (src/scenes/PrologueScene.ts reads it line by line). Working text: drafted with the
// designer and accepted as it stands for now; it can still be rewritten freely. The beats are the designer's:
// Mum tenderly teaching you to carve, praise and hints of your talent, the slip that takes your
// left thumb and forefinger, black, then your older self's narration and how the tree works.
//
// No hands or faces on screen: just the bird, the knife, and the two voices (you and Mum).
// The bird is your first offering, meant to go up the tube at tomorrow's morning rite (designer).
//
// Tags (see PrologueScene): #name:Mum / #name:You (a voice, no face) · #step:turn · #step:smooth
// · #step:carve · #step:eye · #step:slip · #black · #narration · #tableau:tree / #tableau:tube · #end
// ============================================================================

=== prologue ===
"There. Your very first bird." #name:Mum
"Is it really going up? Tomorrow?" #name:You
"At the morning rite, next to mine. Your first offering." #name:Mum
"Hold it gently, like it might fly off. Turn it to the fire so you can see the grain." #name:Mum #step:turn
"Good. See how the grain runs? Always with it, never against." #name:Mum
"Why does it have to be so perfect?" #name:You
"Because it's for the Tree. Everything we send up, it sees." #name:Mum
"Start with the head. Rock it in your hands and let the rough bits fall away." #name:Mum #step:smooth
"Lovely. Feel how smooth that is now?" #name:Mum
"Keep this up and you'll be living in the high branches one day, where it's always warm." #name:Mum
"Me? Up there?" #name:You
"With hands like yours? I wouldn't bet against it." #name:Mum
"Now along the wing. Slowly. Let the blade do the work." #name:Mum #step:carve
"Well done! Look at that line. Better than mine at your age." #name:Mum
"My mother taught me at this same bench. I cried the first time I cut a feather wrong." #name:Mum
"Now the eye. That's what gives it life. One small cut, right in the middle." #name:Mum #step:eye
"There. It's looking at you." #name:Mum
"Will the gods like it?" #name:You
"The gods will love your birds. I know it." #name:Mum
"Turn it round. The other wing, and it's ready for the morning." #name:Mum #step:turn
"Same again, just as slow..." #name:Mum #step:slip
"Mum! Mum, it hurts!" #name:You
"No, no, no! Give it here, give me your hand!" #name:Mum
My first offering never went up the tube. #black #narration
The knife took my left thumb, and the finger beside it. #narration
That day, the roots claimed me for good. #narration
A carver with a ruined hand makes flawed birds. And in the tree, what you make decides where you live. #narration
I still see every bird perfectly in my head. My hands just can't make it. #narration
The tree is fair, they say. You give your work, and it gives back in kind. #tableau:tree #narration
The finest makers rise: better wood, better food, homes in the high branches. The rest stay low. #narration
Each morning our best work goes up the tube. Each evening, blessings come down to those who earned them. #tableau:tube #narration
It is fair. As long as your hands can do what your mind can. #narration #end
-> END
